<section class="era">
  <div class="era-head"><span class="era-no">CASE 12</span><h2 style="margin:0; font-size:1.35rem;">Running a fleet, not a server</h2><span class="era-dates">2025 – 2026</span></div>
  <p class="impact-pill"><span class="lab">Impact</span><span class="val">3 workers → ~120, each one justified</span></p>
  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The problem</span>
    <p>Splitting queues, as Case&nbsp;11 shows, is how you stop unrelated work from queuing behind itself.
    But every split leaves you with something new to run — and by 2026 the platform was no longer "an
    application on a server." It was <strong>23 different queues and roughly 120 worker processes spread
    across three machines</strong>, each one a small daemon holding database connections, consuming memory,
    and capable of ruining the others' day.</p>
    <p>Nobody designs that on day one. It accumulates, and it accumulates asymmetrically: a queue that was
    busy in March is idle in July, a queue nobody predicted becomes the bottleneck overnight, and the two
    servers you sized identically turn out to be doing wildly different amounts of work. Left unmanaged, a
    worker fleet develops exactly the problems a city does — congestion in one district, empty streets in
    another, and infrastructure nobody remembers approving.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">How we manage it</span>
    <p><strong>Every worker count is an argument, not a default.</strong> The fleet is described in one
    place — a deployment script that assigns each queue its worker count per machine — so the whole topology
    is reviewable in a code review rather than discovered by SSH-ing into a box. When a number changes, the
    reason is in the commit.</p>
    <p><strong>We add workers where the measurements point.</strong> The trader-order queue went from 5 to
    10 when timing data showed jobs waiting rather than working. The unit-creation queue was scaled to 8
    and 16 on different machines when it became the new tail of the purchase step. Ownership and auto-sell
    got their own dedicated queues on separate machines (Case&nbsp;11).</p>
    <p><strong>And we remove them just as deliberately.</strong> Dead queues left over from old flows were
    deleted; a misleadingly named worker was renamed to what it actually did — with its old queue drained
    first, because renaming a queue while jobs are still sitting in it is how you lose transactions. One
    queue is deliberately pinned at a <em>single worker</em> forever: the step where parallelism cannot be
    made safe. In a document full of scaling-up decisions, the most important number in the fleet is a
    one.</p>
    <p><strong>The failures here are their own genre.</strong> A duplicate scale flag once silently halved a
    queue's workers — the kind of bug that produces no error, just a slower system. A single broken anchor
    in a compose file blocked <em>every</em> deployment until it was found. And the day the full fleet came
    up at once, it exhausted the database's connection limit — because 120 workers politely holding two
    connections each is 240 connections nobody had budgeted for. Now every environment has an explicit
    connection budget, and smaller environments run deliberately small.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="metric">
    <span class="tag">The result</span>
    The fleet grew from <strong>3 worker containers to ~120 processes across 3 machines</strong> — and
    unlike the platform of 2024, we can say why each one exists. The same discipline now runs in reverse:
    with the workload measured, we are consolidating three machines into two while keeping total queue
    concurrency identical, because the goal was never "more workers." It was <em>the right workers, in the
    right places, for reasons written down.</em>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="era">
  <div class="era-head"><span class="era-no">CASE 13</span><h2 style="margin:0; font-size:1.35rem;">The boring failures</h2><span class="era-dates">Throughout</span></div>
  <p class="impact-pill"><span class="lab">Impact</span><span class="val">surprises moved out of production</span></p>
  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The problem</span>
    <p>Not every scale failure is clever. The stress-test program kept tripping over limits nobody had ever
    written down: a test server's disk at 98% (December 2024), and stress-test environments filling their
    disks with logs again in March 2026. A <code>smallint</code> column that overflowed when test data
    pushed past <strong>32,000 registered companies</strong> — the database equivalent of an odometer
    rolling over. The default 60-second job timeout. The day the full worker fleet exhausted the database's
    connections. Deploys that killed whatever jobs were running.</p>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/blog/scaling-murabaha-10-to-5000-orders/figure-5.gif" alt="The 'This is fine' dog sitting calmly in a burning room with a cup of coffee."
         style="max-width: 22rem; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px;">
    <figcaption>The platform at 98% disk usage, moments before our first stuck-orders investigation.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">What we tried</span>
    <p>Each one got the boring fix it deserved: standardized log rotation, wider column types, explicit
    connection budgets per environment (smaller environments hold ~28 database connections instead of ~86 at
    full scale), graceful worker shutdown. But the real fix is the habit around them: the stress tests run
    <strong>daily</strong> precisely so that the next limit nobody wrote down fails in a test, in
    pre-production, at dawn — instead of in production, at noon, with customers watching.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="metric">
    <span class="tag">The result</span>
    The last classes of surprise moved from production incidents to test findings. What still strands at
    5,000-order scale — a handful of orders per campaign — is handled operationally: an audited per-step
    "Force Forward" action for admins, with automatic order expiry as the second safety net.
  </div>
</section>


